CHARTVOLT
Preliminary Legal Opinion
CONFIDENTIAL • 13 OCTOBER 2026 • CYPRUS / EUROPEAN UNION / INTERNATIONAL MARKETS

Subject: Regulatory classification of ChartVolt's paid-entry game competitions, 1v1 challenges, simulated-trading competitions, pooled prizes and cash-redeemable Volts.

Purpose: To state the strongest legally sustainable argument for operating ChartVolt without a betting, gambling, investment-services, payment-institution or electronic-money licence, to state honestly where that argument is weak, and to list the changes that would strengthen it.

This is a preliminary analysis based on published legislation and regulatory guidance. It is not a formal legal opinion from a Cyprus-qualified lawyer and must be reviewed and adopted by Cyprus counsel before being relied on or shown to a regulator. Every factual statement about the platform was checked against the ChartVolt source code on 13 October 2026; where a statement is a design intention rather than a verified fact, it says so.

1. Executive conclusion — the honest answer first

No lawyer can truthfully promise that ChartVolt needs "no licence of any kind" in Cyprus and across the EU. Gambling is not harmonised at EU level, each Member State applies its own test, and two features of the current platform give a regulator a real foothold. What can be built is a strong, documented position for each service separately, and the platform already does more of that than most operators at this stage.

Area | Current position | Risk
Simulated trading / MiFID II | Strongest. No real instruments, prizes come from the entry-fee pool, not from simulated P&L | Lower
Cyprus "betting" definition | Good argument: no odds, no bookmaker, no wager on external events, no house exposure | Medium
Circuit Sprint, Volt Stack (games of skill) | Strong: identical seeded content for every competitor, no paid hints/retries, ranked by score | Medium-low
Volt Velocity (live combat race) | Weakest game: weapons that affect other racers, seeded start-grid shuffle, real-time network conditions | High
Electronic money | Arguable: Volts are accepted only by ChartVolt for its own services | Medium
Payment services / stored value | Weak today: purchased Volts can be withdrawn without being used; Game Masters receive Volts | Medium-high
Cross-border EU | Each country decides separately | High

These are qualitative estimates, not probabilities or regulatory determinations.

Principal conclusion: A no-licence position is defensible for simulated trading and for the deterministic skill games, and arguable for Volts. It is not defensible today, with confidence, for paid Volt Velocity races or for the unrestricted withdrawal of purchased Volts. Both are fixable by product changes, listed in section 9.

2. The ChartVolt operating model (verified against the code)

Competitions: Users buy Volts and spend them to enter paid competitions (two or more players) or 1v1 challenges (exactly two players). A platform fee is deducted and the remainder funds the prize pool. ChartVolt does not add money to the pool, does not take the other side of any result, and does not set odds. ChartVolt earns the same fee whoever wins.

No single-player paid format: Every paid format has at least two players. A competition that does not reach its minimum is cancelled with full refunds. The only one-player mode is free practice, with no prize and no ranking.

Ranking: Winners are ranked by each title's published score and score direction (for example, higher score wins, or fastest time wins). The direction is fixed per title in the catalogue, not chosen by the game provider per result.

Game providers never touch money: An external or first-party game service reports a score only. Fees, winners, payouts and refunds are decided by ChartVolt's settlement engine.

Players with no result: For each contest the operator chooses, in advance, whether a player who recorded no score is refunded or excluded. Cancelled and failed competitions are refunded in full.

Marketplace rule: Nothing sold may improve a score or ranking in a paid contest. No paid hints, retries, extra time, skips or easier content. No random or mystery packs, even cosmetic ones.

Simulated trading: Participants receive fictional capital. No real instruments are bought, no orders are routed to a market, and simulated profit is never paid out. The prize is the competition pool, allocated by rank.

The current game catalogue:
Title | What the player does | How content is chosen | Status
