Final assessment: The truthful position is not "ChartVolt needs no licence of any kind". It is this. ChartVolt has a strong case that its simulated trading is not an investment service, that it is not a betting operator, and that its deterministic skill games are games of skill. It has an arguable case that Volts are not e-money. It has two concrete weaknesses: paid combat racing, and the cash-out of purchased Volts. Both can be removed by product decisions within ChartVolt's control. Once they are, the remaining risk is mainly about which countries to enter, and that is managed market by market.
